SOURCE
id: 1
ts: 2026-05-29T00:01:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: About data flow analysis - CodeQL
authors: GitHub/CodeQL team
year: 2024
venue: codeql.github.com (official documentation)
quality: DOCS
url: https://codeql.github.com/docs/writing-codeql-queries/about-data-flow-analysis/
keywords: CodeQL, dataflow, taint tracking, source, sink, sanitizer, barrier, global dataflow, local dataflow, path-problem
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  Data flow graph nodes represent semantic elements carrying values at runtime (distinct from AST nodes).
  Edges represent how data flows between program elements.
  LOCAL dataflow: edges between nodes in the SAME function; faster, lower memory.
  GLOBAL dataflow: tracks across function boundaries, through object properties; higher cost.
  NORMAL dataflow: preserves exact values end-to-end.
  TAINT TRACKING: extends dataflow; includes steps where values are not preserved but
    "potentially insecure object is still propagated."
  Configuration predicates:
    isSource(Node n) — marks source nodes
    isSink(Node n) — marks sink nodes
    isBarrier(Node n) — unified sanitizer/barrier for both taint and dataflow configs
    isAdditionalFlowStep(Node n1, Node n2) — adds custom edges
  Query format for path output:
    @kind path-problem metadata tag required
    MyFlow::flowPath(source, sink) predicate reports flow paths
    SELECT includes: sink node, source path node, sink path node, descriptive text
  Limitations acknowledged: unavailable source code, runtime-determined behavior,
    variable aliasing, computational complexity.
  Flow state labels (FlowLabel / FlowState) allow distinguishing taint kinds along a path.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 2
ts: 2026-05-29T00:01:30Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Taint analysis overview - Semgrep
authors: Semgrep team
year: 2024
venue: semgrep.dev (official documentation)
quality: DOCS
url: https://semgrep.dev/docs/writing-rules/data-flow/taint-mode/overview
keywords: Semgrep, taint, dataflow, source, sink, sanitizer, propagator, YAML, interfile, cross-file, taint trace
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  Rule structure requires mode: taint in YAML.
  Operators:
    pattern-sources (required): patterns identifying tainted data origins
    pattern-sinks (optional): vulnerable function call patterns
    pattern-sanitizers (optional): patterns marking cleaned data
    pattern-propagators (optional): explicit taint propagation rules;
      require 'from' and 'to' metavariable fields
  Source configuration options:
    exact: true/false (default false) — whether subexpressions count as sources
    by-side-effect: true/false (default false) — side-effect-based tainting
    control: true/false (Pro feature) — control flow sources
  Sink options differ: default exact: true (subexpressions not auto-flagged).
    at-exit: Pro feature, restricts detection to exit points.
  Findings: "accompanied by a taint trace that explains how the taint flows from source to sink."
  Deduplication: one representative trace per sink reported even when multiple paths exist.
  Interprocedural (Pro): --pro-intrafile (within files) or --pro with interfile: true (across files).
  No confidence/severity fields documented in this page.
  Version 1.92.0 (2024-10-17): added Pro support to track taint through callbacks leading to a sink.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 3
ts: 2026-05-29T00:02:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Pysa (Static Analysis for Security) — Basics
authors: Meta/Facebook Pyre team
year: 2020 (ongoing)
venue: pyre-check.org (official documentation)
quality: DOCS
url: https://pyre-check.org/docs/pysa-basics/
keywords: Pysa, taint analysis, Python, source, sink, sanitizer, TaintSource, TaintSink, TaintInTaintOut, .pysa model files, taint.config, interprocedural
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  Two config file types:
    taint.config (JSON): declares source kinds, sink kinds, features, rules
    .pysa model files: annotate code with source/sink/sanitizer roles
  Location: taint_models_path in .pyre_configuration
  Source declaration (taint.config):
    {"sources": [{"name": "Cookies", "comment": "..."}]}
  Annotation in .pysa:
    TaintSource[SOURCE_NAME] on function returns, class attributes, or whole classes
  Sink annotation:
    TaintSink[SINK_NAME] on function parameters and attributes
  Path annotation for indexable types:
    ReturnPath[_[0][1]] for nested list/tuple indexes
    ReturnPath[_["a"]] for dict keys
  Sanitizer annotations:
    @Sanitize — clears all taint
    @Sanitize(Parameters) — all arguments sanitized
    @Sanitize(TaintInTaintOut) — prevents taint propagation through returns
    Sanitize[TaintSource[UserControlled]] — specific source type
    Sanitize[TaintInTaintOut[TaintSink[SQL]]] — TITO with specific sinks
  TaintInTaintOut propagation:
    TaintInTaintOut[LocalReturn] — tainted args produce tainted returns
    TaintInTaintOut[Updates[PARAMETER]] — param updates from tainted data
  Rules in taint.config:
    {
      "name": "SQL injection.",
      "code": 1,
      "sources": ["UserControlled"],
      "sinks": ["SQL"],
      "message_format": "Data from [{$sources}] source(s)..."
    }
    Each rule: unique code, source/sink lists, message templates with {$sources}/{$sinks} vars.
  Interprocedural scope: only analyzes repository code + search_path directories.
    External dependencies without source: assumes function returns inherit taint from inputs
    (can cause false positives).
  "When an object is tainted, all attributes of that object are also tainted" — known FP source.
  Model requirements: fully qualified names, signature must match Pyre's understanding exactly,
    type annotations must be completely removed from .pysa files.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 4
ts: 2026-05-29T00:02:30Z
relevance: HIGH
title: IRIS: LLM-Assisted Static Analysis for Detecting Security Vulnerabilities (arXiv 2405.17238)
authors: (from abstract page)
year: 2024
venue: arXiv preprint
quality: PREPRINT
url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2405.17238
keywords: IRIS, LLM, static analysis, taint specification inference, CodeQL, vulnerability detection, CWE-Bench-Java, neuro-symbolic
cites: CodeQL
cited_by:
content:
  Approach: neuro-symbolic combining LLMs with static analysis for whole-repository vulnerability detection.
  Problem addressed: static analysis tools rely on human-labeled taint specs; LLMs can't do
    complex reasoning over code for vulnerability detection at repository scale.
  Architecture:
    LLMs infer taint specifications (source/sink annotations) — reduces dependence on human-written rules.
    Static analysis performs contextual examination of code using inferred specs.
  Benchmark: CWE-Bench-Java — 120 manually validated security vulnerabilities from production Java projects.
  Results:
    CodeQL baseline: detected 27/120 vulnerabilities.
    IRIS with GPT-4: detected 55/120 vulnerabilities (+28 improvement, ~2x baseline).
    False discovery rate: 5 percentage point improvement over CodeQL baseline.
    Novel findings: 4 previously unknown vulnerabilities identified.
  Key insight: LLMs infer specs; static analysis verifies paths — division of labor cuts hallucinations.
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 5
ts: 2026-05-29T00:03:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: Lessons from Building Static Analysis Tools at Google (CACM 2018)
authors: Caitlin Sadowski, Edward Aftandilian, Alex Eagle, Liam Miller-Cushon, Ciera Jaspan
year: 2018
venue: Communications of the ACM, Vol. 61 No. 4
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://cacm.acm.org/research/lessons-from-building-static-analysis-tools-at-google/
keywords: Tricorder, Google, static analysis, false positive, code review, developer experience, suppression, feedback, 10% rule
cites:
cited_by: widely cited in static analysis UX literature
content:
  Key false positive metrics:
    Overall effective false-positive rate for Tricorder: just below 5%.
    Code review checks: allowed up to 10% effective false positives.
    Compiler-time checks: stricter threshold (lower than code review).
  False positive definition: "any report [users] did not want to see" — user-centric, not purely technical.
  UI mechanism: "NOT USEFUL" button on findings in code review; continuous monitoring of rates.
  Tricorder scope: 30+ languages; syntactic style checks; compiler-informed checks for Java/JS/C++.
  Integration point: results shown during code review (not as blocking gate).
  Key lesson: showing results at code review time (not build time) dramatically improves fix rate.
  Developer buy-in: analyzers must maintain <10% FP rate or they get turned off.
  Suppression mechanism: per-finding suppression with rationale; feeds back to analyzer teams.
  Fix-it ergonomics: automated fix suggestions shown alongside findings; one-click apply.
  Source: PDF also at https://6826.csail.mit.edu/2020/papers/google-analysis-cacm.pdf
<<<END>>>

