SOURCE
id: 15
ts: 2026-05-29T00:15:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: LLMDFA: Analyzing Dataflow in Code with Large Language Models — Full Details
authors: Chengpeng Wang et al.
year: 2024
venue: NeurIPS 2024 (Poster); arXiv 2402.10754
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.10754
keywords: LLMDFA, LLM, dataflow, compilation-free, few-shot, chain-of-thought, theorem prover, path feasibility, hallucination, bug detection, Android
cites:
cited_by: LLM4PFA (arXiv 2506.10322)
content:
  Problem: traditional dataflow analysis requires successful compilation and expert customization;
    uncompilable programs and evolving analysis needs hinder applicability.
  Core novelty: compilation-free dataflow analysis powered by LLMs.
  Anti-hallucination techniques:
    1. Task decomposition: problem broken into subtasks.
    2. Code synthesis: LLM generates code to outsource delicate reasoning to external tools
       (e.g., parsing libraries to extract program values).
    3. Theorem prover integration: automated theorem prover validates path feasibility.
    4. Few-shot chain-of-thought prompting: summarizes dataflow facts per individual function,
       aligning LLM outputs with program semantics.
  Results:
    Precision: 87.10% average.
    Recall: 80.77% average.
    F1 improvement: up to 0.35 over existing techniques.
  Evaluation: synthetic programs (3 bug types) + real-world Android apps (customized bug detection).
  Code open-sourced: https://github.com/chengpeng-wang/LLMDFA
  Paper: 26 pages, 15 figures, 6 tables.
  Key insight: LLM synthesizes code that calls parsers/provers rather than directly reasoning about paths.
<<<END>>>

