title: Augmenting LLMs with Static Code Analysis for Automated Code Quality Improvements (arXiv 2506.10330)
authors: (arXiv 2506.10330)
year: 2025
venue: arXiv preprint
quality: PREPRINT
url: https://arxiv.org/html/2506.10330v1
keywords: SonarQube, LLM, GPT-4o, GPT-3.5, RAG, code smell, bug, vulnerability, static analysis, prompt engineering, retrieval augmented generation
cites: SonarQube
cited_by:
content:
  Static analysis tool used: SonarQube (identifies bugs, vulnerabilities, code smells).
  Issue extraction: file names, line numbers, issue descriptions, suggested solutions from SonarQube.
  Data organized into files for structured LLM processing.
  Models tested: GPT-3.5 Turbo and GPT-4o.
  Prompt engineering: 20+ iterations; two-step approach:
    Step 1: specify programming language, delineate task.
    Step 2: few-shot learning with examples per issue type.
  RAG: retrieves solutions from Stack Overflow, GitHub, SonarQube Community, Google.
    Solutions ranked by: source reliability, relevance, redundancy.
    Integrated into enriched prompts.
  Hallucination mitigation: "Code Comparison App" identifies erroneous changes before implementation.
  Results:
    Bugs: 234 total, 100% combined success rate, F1 98.4%.
    Vulnerabilities: 61 total, 100% combined success rate, F1 99.25%.
    Code smells: 7304 total, 81.2% combined success rate, F1 96.05%.
    GPT-4o outperformed GPT-3.5 Turbo across all categories.
    Cost: less than $35 for 7,599+ issues in under 3 hours.
  Divided approach (issue types separately) outperforms comprehensive processing.
<<<END>>>

