  Scale: millions of lines of code at Facebook.
  Bug types: null dereferences, data races, security issues.
  Key metric: for null dereferences, data races, and security issues, 
    "over 50% of fixes were for bugs with traces that were interprocedural."
  False positive issue: Infer fails to detect that null has been checked via Objects.nonNull() 
    vs explicit null checks — implementation-specific FP source.
  Developer experience: FPs documented in GitHub issues #1642, #1644.
  Production deployment: "avoiding false positives and giving only relevant warnings."
  (Additional detail from: https://engineering.fb.com/2017/09/06/android/finding-inter-procedural-bugs-at-scale-with-infer-static-analyzer/)
<<<END>>>

SOURCE
id: 23
ts: 2026-05-29T00:19:00Z
relevance: HIGH
title: An Empirical Study of Suppressed Static Analysis Warnings (FSE 2025)
authors: (software-lab.org/publications/fse2025_suppressions.pdf)
year: 2025
venue: FSE 2025
quality: PEER-REVIEWED
url: https://software-lab.org/publications/fse2025_suppressions.pdf
keywords: suppression, static analysis, false positive, developer behavior, technical debt, warning, triage
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  Key findings on suppression behavior:
    False positives are the most common reason for using suppressions (34.4% of all cases).
    Developers "mostly add suppressions along with new code, in an explicit effort to reduce new warnings."
    Over half of all suppressions are useless — they do not suppress any warning.
    A significant number of suppressions introduce technical debt.
  Reasons developers suppress:
    False positives from incomplete language/framework support.
    Imprecise and misleading messages.
    Improper configurations.
    Warnings deemed not important enough.
  Root cause of high FP: tools are "largely path-insensitive"; manual triage of large numbers of false alarms
    "significantly reducing the practical utility of the tool."
  Recommendation: better communication/education on SA tools, improved bug pattern definitions,
    better code annotation support.
<<<END>>>

