relevance: HIGH
title: LLM Hallucinations in Code Review — Systematic Overcorrection (arXiv 2603.00539)
authors: (arXiv 2603.00539)
year: 2026
venue: arXiv preprint
quality: PREPRINT
url: https://arxiv.org/pdf/2603.00539
keywords: LLM, code review, hallucination, overcorrection, false positive, over-reporting, requirement conformance
cites:
cited_by:
content:
  Key finding: "increasing prompt complexity substantially reduces models' Requirement Conformance 
    Recognition Rate."
  Overcorrection bias: correct implementations misclassified as non-conforming.
  Detailed prompts "may inadvertently introduce biases toward excessive fault finding."
  Models "detect non-existent errors in otherwise correct implementations."
  Implication for flow-graph reviewers: more complex prompts (e.g., including full dataflow paths) 
    may cause over-reporting of false findings.
  Title: "Are LLMs Reliable Code Reviewers? Systematic Overcorrection in Requirement Conformance Judgement"
<<<END>>>

